  "concept": "The sine of an angle is the height (y-coordinate) of the corresponding point on the unit circle, so the graph of y = sin(x) is a running record of that height as the angle turns.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: faint gray unit circle centered at origin with horizontal and vertical axis arrows through it",
    "Left panel: fan of faint gray radii drawn at the special angles (pi/6, pi/4, pi/3, pi/2 and their reflections around the circle)",
    "Left panel: highlighted dark-blue radius from center to the moving point on the circle at the current angle x",
    "Left panel: moving point on the circle, with a short vertical drop segment from the point to the horizontal axis (its height)",
    "Right panel: Cartesian axes, horizontal axis labeled x with arrowheads, vertical axis labeled y with arrowhead",
    "Right panel: y-axis tick labels 1, 0, -1 with small tick marks",
    "Right panel: x-axis tick marks labeled pi/6, pi/4, pi/3, pi/2 (stacked fraction labels below the axis)",
    "Right panel: solid dark blue curve y = sin(x) entering from lower left with an arrowhead, through the origin, rising to peak 1, turning down to a second arrowhead",
    "Right panel: curve label 'y = sin(x)' in blue near the peak",
    "Right panel: four dashed orange vertical segments from the curve down to the labeled x-axis ticks",
    "Dashed teal horizontal connector lines running from points on the sine curve leftward across the gap to the matching heights on the unit circle",
    "Moving marker dot on the sine curve at (x, sin x) matching the circle point",
    "Readout label showing current angle x and value sin(x)"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "angle",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Angle x (radians)",
      "range": [
        0,
        6.283,
        0.01
      ],
      "default": 0.5236,
      "teaches": "The point on the circle and the point on the graph are the same fact told twice: the circle point's height IS the graph's y-value at that angle."
