    {
      "id": "quarterSweep",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Sweep the radius from 0 to pi/2",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "The sine curve is drawn in real time as the running record of the circle point's rising height through the first quadrant."
    },
    {
      "id": "specialAngles",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Special-angle markers",
      "options": [
        "show",
        "hide"
      ],
      "default": "show",
      "teaches": "The familiar values sin(pi/6)=1/2, sin(pi/4)=√2/2, sin(pi/3)=√3/2, sin(pi/2)=1 are just four heights read off the same circle."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Two pictures, one function",
      "narration": "On the left a point rides the unit circle; on the right the same information is plotted against the angle. The dashed teal line connects them at the same height.",
      "state": {
        "angle": 0.5236,
        "specialAngles": "show"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read the special angles",
      "narration": "Drag to pi/6, pi/4, pi/3: each orange drop line marks the angle on the x-axis while the teal line marks the matching height on the circle — 1/2, √2/2, √3/2.",
      "state": {
        "angle": 1.0472,
        "specialAngles": "show"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "The quarter-turn peak",
      "narration": "At x = pi/2 the radius points straight up, the circle point's height is the full radius 1, and the graph reaches its maximum.",
      "state": {
        "angle": 1.5708,
        "specialAngles": "show"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch the record being written",
      "narration": "Play the sweep: as the radius rotates from 0 to pi/2, the curve is traced from the origin to its peak — the graph is the height, plotted moment by moment.",
      "state": {
        "angle": 1.5708,
        "specialAngles": "hide"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Past the peak, and below the axis",
      "narration": "Keep dragging past pi/2: the height shrinks back to 0 at pi, then goes negative in the third and fourth quadrants — quadrant position explains the sign of sine.",
      "state": {
        "angle": 4.1888,
        "specialAngles": "hide"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "sin(x) is not a new quantity to memorize — it is literally the vertical height of the unit-circle point at angle x, and the sine graph is that height plotted against the angle.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two panels side by side on a white background. Left: a faint gray unit circle with gray axis arrows and a fan of thin gray radii at the special angles; the active radius, point, and its vertical height segment are dark blue. Right: black axes with arrowheads, y ticks labeled 1, 0, -1, x ticks labeled with stacked fractions pi/6, pi/4, pi/3, pi/2 placed close together near the left of the axis. The sine curve is a thick dark navy-blue arc entering from the lower left with an arrowhead, crossing the origin, peaking at 1 above pi/2, then descending to a second arrowhead near x slightly past the peak; label 'y = sin(x)' in blue above the peak. Dashed orange vertical segments drop from the curve to the four labeled ticks. Dashed teal horizontal lines run from those curve points leftward across the blank gap between panels to the same heights on the gray circle, emphasizing equal height. The active angle's teal connector and blue markers should be visually dominant; the four static special-angle dashes are secondary and toggleable."
}

